    "content" : "4lin, speciﬁc growth yield during linear growth. Modes: a, one impeller mounted; b, two impellers mounted. YCH"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 50,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 391.403, 532.081, 428.057, 541.318 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 7.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "4max and YCH"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 1,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 43.165, 374.388, 283.245, 496.051 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 9.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "time, the slope representing the speciﬁc growth rate during the exponential phase ( ex) (see also the supplemental material [1]). Deviation from linearity was always seen in the period preceding the exponential phase (lag period). Here, perfect straight lines were obtained, if the ln(OD600) values were plotted against the squared times (Fig. 3A, inset). As outlined in in the supplemental material, this implies that the speciﬁc growth during the lag phase increases linearly in time (Fig. 1B and 2B). Also, the speciﬁc methane-forming activity increased linearly in time during this period (Fig. 1B and 2B). In fact, a plot of the qCH"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 3,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 43.164, 341.388, 283.237, 386.051 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 9.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "values against the corresponding values showed a direct proportional relationship between both parameters, the slope representing the reciprocal of the speciﬁc growth yield (YCH"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 2,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 81.039, 374.158, 83.067, 379.103 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 4.057,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "4"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 5,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 43.165, 297.388, 283.247, 353.051 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 9.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "), which now was constant (Fig. 1C, 2C, and 3A). The physiological meaning of these ﬁndings is that, after inoculation, cells adapt to the new growth condition by the uniform and concerted acceleration of their speciﬁc growth rate and speciﬁc methane-forming activity in a way that YCH"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 4,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 60.511, 341.158, 62.539, 346.103 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 4.057,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "4"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 43.165, 287.08, 283.234, 309.051 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 9.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "remains ﬁxed."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 248.401, 297.157, 250.429, 302.103 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 4.057,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "4"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 43.165, 220.388, 283.24, 287.051 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 9.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "The speciﬁc growth rates became maximal at the entry of the exponential stage and remained constant throughout the particular stage (Fig. 1B, 2B, and 3A). The values did not significantly differ among the various gassing regimens (0.22 to 0.25 h 1; doubling times of 2.8 to 3.1 h) (Table 3). However, the qCH"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 10,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 43.165, 155.081, 283.247, 232.051 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 9.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "was not constant. Speciﬁc methane-forming activities initially increased to a maximum and declined thereafter. The decline occurred concomitant with the decrease in the dissolved hydrogen partial pressures, resulting from the increasing hydrogen uptake rates by the growing biomass (Fig. 1 and 2 and Table 3). Apparently, the physiology of the cells continuously changed, even at a constant growth rate."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 9,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.092, 220.157, 58.12, 225.103 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 4.057,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "4"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 11,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 43.165, 99.388, 283.231, 155.052 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 9.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "After exponential growth, the cells entered the linear phase. During this stage, the speciﬁc growth rates and speciﬁc methane-forming activities, calculated by using the equations S.5 and S.6, respectively, in the supplemental material(1), decreased in parallel (Fig. 1B and 2B). Moreover, qCH"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 13,
    "level" : "1",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 247.475, 100.08, 277.202, 111.051 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 9.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "-versus-"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 12,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 245.447, 99.158, 247.475, 104.103 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 4.057,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "4"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 14,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 43.165, 56.08, 283.242, 100.051 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 9.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "plots showed the direct proportional relationship as expected from equation S.8 (Fig. 1C, 2C, and 3B). The slope of the plots, which equals the reciprocal of the speciﬁc growth yield (equation S.8), varied with the dissolved hydrogen partial pressure"
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 15,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 324.955, 159.886, 516.955, 483.886 ],
    "alt_source" : "missing",
    "source" : "027880a87e3c2ad4cf8c05f477597c0c6da7_page_7_pg1_images/imageFile1.png"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 16,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 301.164, 73.356, 541.25, 156.09 ],
    "font" : "Dutch801BT-Roman",
    "font size" : 8.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "FIG. 3. Relationship between the speciﬁc rates of methanogenesis and speciﬁc growth rates during the lag (A), linear (B), and exponential growth phases. M. thermautotrophicus was cultured in 2.5 (A) and 1.5 (B) liters of mineral medium at a constant gassing rate of 428 ml min 1 in 80% H2–20% CO2 (vol/vol). Measurements started 5 h (A) and 8 h (B) after adjustment of the gassing rates. In the inset of Fig. 3A, the ln(OD600) is plotted versus the squared time after inoculation. Lag ( ), exponential (Œ), and linear (}) growth stages are as indicated. ex, speciﬁc growth rate during the exponential phase;"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 17,
